<% content_for :title, "Triggers | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="addiction-triggers-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section
    class="addiction-triggers-hero"
    style="
      --triggers-mobile: url('<%= asset_path("triggers_mobile_background.png") %>');
      --triggers-desktop: url('<%= asset_path("triggers_desktop_background.png") %>');
    "
  >
    <div class="addiction-triggers-hero-content">

      <p class="addiction-triggers-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ADDICTION / TRIGGERS
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>ON</span>
        <span>TRIGGERS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="addiction-triggers-intro">
        THE PAIN. THE MOODS. THE FUCK-ITS.<br>
        KNOW WHAT SETS YOU OFF.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / OPENING -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-pink">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">01 / HANDLE WITH CARE</p>

      <h2>
        KNOW THE<br>
        TRIGGER.<br>
        RESPECT IT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <blockquote>
        “You must unlearn what you have learned.”
        <br><br>
        — Yoda
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        We were on private property up County Road 87J,
        shooting at paper targets in the woods. After carefully
        aiming a small pistol, I proudly hit the target.
        Richard had told me it had no safety and a hair trigger,
        so I handled it gingerly and returned it with
        deliberate care.
      </p>

      <p class="at-lead">
        I keep that attitude about my recovery.
      </p>

      <p>
        A bottle, a backache, a boring evening, or a character
        cracking open a beer on TV. Nice weather or a snowstorm.
        Being alone or being around friends. Triggers can show
        up in ordinary life and inside your own head.
      </p>

      <p>
        Knowing yours means noticing what happens before
        drinking starts sounding like a great idea.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / PAIN -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-yellow">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">02 / PHYSICAL PAIN</p>

      <h2>
        YOU NEED<br>
        RELIEF.<br>
        NOW.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p class="at-lead">
        Pain is your biggest trigger.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your back hurts, your legs burn, your ankle feels
        on fire, and your foot goes numb. You calculate how
        long you can stand or walk and whether doing something
        now will leave you hurting later.
      </p>

      <p>
        Relief feels inadequate, appointments are days away,
        and you are tired of waiting. Alcohol starts promising
        a break from your body demanding attention.
      </p>

      <p>
        The restrictions add their own weight. You miss walking,
        riding a bicycle, skateboarding, and finishing a task
        without negotiating with pain. Watching other people
        move easily through their lives can feel infuriating.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        NOTICE WHEN “I NEED RELIEF”<br>
        BECOMES “I DESERVE A DRINK.”
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Your need for relief deserves attention. Use your
        established pain plan and tell your care team when
        it is not enough. Let someone know when pain begins
        turning into permission to drink.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / MOOD EPISODES -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-purple">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">03 / MOOD EPISODES</p>

      <h2>
        TOO LOW.<br>
        TOO FAST.<br>
        TOO MUCH.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p>
        Depression can last for months. You move through
        the same rooms with the same heaviness, wanting
        something to anticipate and an evening that feels
        different. Alcohol seems to offer that change.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mania brings racing thoughts, relentless activity,
        and impulsivity. You get the fuck-its, and the
        reasons to wait become harder to hold onto.
        Drinking can start sounding like a way to settle
        everything down.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mixed symptoms can leave you miserable and pacing,
        wanting to flee without knowing where you would go.
        Your thoughts will not stop, and you cannot
        comfortably sit with yourself.
      </p>

      <p class="at-lead">
        Sometimes the trigger is the urgency itself:
        something has to change right fucking now.
      </p>

      <p>
        Recognize what alcohol is promising without assuming
        it can safely deliver it. Tell your treatment team
        about changes in your mood and impulses, and involve
        support before the urge becomes a plan.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / ANGER AND BOREDOM -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-black">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">04 / ANGRY OR RESTLESS</p>

      <h2>
        FUCK THIS.<br>
        NOW WHAT?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p>
        Traffic, a broken website, code that will not work,
        or a task that should have been simple can become
        the final inconvenience you cannot stand.
        You start replaying everything else that has gone wrong.
      </p>

      <p>
        Notice when frustration with one situation becomes
        a verdict on the entire day. The anger builds,
        and you want something to calm you down immediately.
      </p>

      <p>
        Boredom belongs here, too. You do not need a crisis
        to want a drink. Sometimes you are tired of being home,
        tired of your options, and ready for something to happen.
      </p>

      <p>
        Pain makes that harder when walking, exercising,
        or getting out are not realistic options.
        When you cannot go anywhere, it can seem easier
        to teleport your head someplace instead.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        WHAT WOULD HELP YOU FEEL<br>
        LESS TRAPPED TONIGHT?
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / CONTROL AND UNCERTAINTY -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-pink">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">05 / LOSING YOUR SAY</p>

      <h2>
        YOUR LIFE.<br>
        SOMEONE<br>
        ELSE’S<br>
        SCHEDULE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p>
        Depending on someone for a ride or practical help
        means their availability can determine what you do
        and when. A changed plan can bring disappointment,
        anxiety, and the feeling that your choices
        are disappearing.
      </p>

      <p>
        Uncertainty adds another layer. Health, money,
        finding a job, and the future leave you with questions
        you cannot answer. You want to know things will
        improve—and when.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can spend hours trying to think your way
        into certainty. Drinking starts sounding like
        a way to stop the questions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ask what is within reach today: one practical action,
        a backup plan, an honest conversation, or a break
        from trying to solve the whole future tonight.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / SHAME AND THE BUILDUP -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-yellow">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">06 / THE BUILDUP</p>

      <h2>
        IT ADDS<br>
        THE FUCK<br>
        UP.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p>
        Shame can become a trigger long after an event.
        You remember money spent, decisions made,
        or consequences you cannot undo. Then you talk
        to yourself like someone you cannot stand.
        You want distance from that voice.
      </p>

      <p>
        Triggers can accumulate. Pain interrupts your sleep.
        You have less patience. A plan falls through.
        You feel stuck, start criticizing yourself,
        and remember alcohol as an easy way to change
        the evening.
      </p>

      <p class="at-lead">
        By the time you notice the craving,
        several things may already be feeding it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Listen for the language of permission:
        you deserve a break, nobody would blame you,
        just tonight, you will deal with it tomorrow.
        Fuck it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Those thoughts are worth taking seriously.
        Tell someone when you notice yourself making
        the case for drinking.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 / RECOGNITION AND RESPONSE -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-purple">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">07 / LEARN YOUR PATTERNS</p>

      <h2>
        CATCH THE<br>
        BUILDUP.<br>
        GET SUPPORT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p>
        Keep a brief account of what happened before an urge:
        where you were, what you felt, what you told yourself,
        and what you wanted drinking to change.
      </p>

      <p>
        Look for patterns with curiosity. You are gathering
        information that can help you recognize the buildup
        earlier next time.
      </p>

      <p>
        Pain needs care. Loneliness may need connection.
        A concerning mood change needs your treatment team’s
        attention. Bargaining is a reason to use the supports
        and relapse plans you have already prepared.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        YOU DO NOT HAVE TO WAIT<br>
        UNTIL YOU DECIDE TO DRINK<br>
        BEFORE ASKING FOR HELP.
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        You will not catch everything early. When you do
        recognize a trigger, let it guide your response
        instead of becoming another reason to judge yourself.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="at-ending-small">
        KNOW WHAT IS HAPPENING.
      </p>

      <h2>
        NOTICE IT.<br>
        NAME IT.<br>
        <span>RESPOND.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="at-ending-copy">

      <p>
        You do not have to eliminate every difficult feeling
        or avoid ordinary life to protect your recovery.
      </p>

      <p>
        Learn what makes drinking feel appealing
        and what you need during those moments.
      </p>

      <p>
        Start with the experience in front of you.
        Give yourself room to choose your next step
        with support.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO ADDICTION",
          addiction_path,
          class: "at-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>